{"critique": "The draft is confused and partly wrong: it claims \footnote can only be used one way, gives misleading advice about \footnotetext, and its example doesn't actually place a footnote mark or work as described. It's padded with emoji headers and repeated code, and never addresses the real causes (footnotes inside tabular cells, or wanting table-local notes).", "revision": "The usual cause: a `\footnote` inside a `tabular` cell often doesn't print, because LaTeX can't place the mark reliably inside the table box. Two common fixes:

**Use `\footnotemark` + `\footnotetext`** (works with `threeparttable` or plain `table`):

```latex
\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{cc}
\hline
A & 12\footnotemark[1] \\
B & 7 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\footnotetext[1]{Value measured in 2023.}
\caption{Data}\end{table}
```

**Or use the `threeparttable` package**, which is designed for table notes:

```latex
\usepackage{threeparttable}
\begin{table}
\begin{threeparttable}
\begin{tabular}{cc}
A & 12\tnote{a} \\
B & 7 \\
\end{tabular}
\begin{tablenotes}
\item[a] Value measured in 2023.
\end{tablenotes}
\end{threeparttable}
\end{table}
```

`threeparttable` puts the notes at the bottom of the table, not the page. Plain `\footnote` always goes to the page bottom, so if you want table-local notes, `threeparttable` (or `c tablefootnote` for a quick patch) is the right tool.

If it's still missing entirely, check you're not inside a `minipage` or `\parbox` without a footnote mechanism — those swallow footnotes too."}

